From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: acqxi <kacqxi@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 27 Nov 2026 04:17:41 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 12/12] WSL host bridge: controls in Connections and on the
 Environments map

The Environments map draws the bridge as its own kind of link from WSL to
Windows: a violet line, wider than a native link, labeled "Host bridge" with
its state on the edge. Selecting the edge or either machine shows the same
controls as Settings > Connections: Enable and pair, Pair again, Disable,
Revoke, access and collapsed diagnostics. Dragging WSL onto Windows opens
them instead of the ordinary link flow.

States come from the Desktop helper and the WSL backend's native link list:
Discovering, Waiting for Windows or WSL, Bridge starting, Pairing required,
Connected, Recovering, Permission denied, Identity mismatch, Disabled. What
blocks pairing (the WSL backend off or disconnected, Linked environments off
on either side, a runtime without the bridge) shows before Enable.

Enable turns the bridge on, waits for the helper to listen, mints a code on
Windows and links WSL to it, with each step, a progress bar and the elapsed
time shown. A failure names the reason and the next step, and lands in Last
error. Nothing pairs on its own.
